{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-101-introduction-to-civil-and-environm_calendar","statement":"The instructors for the course are Prof. Heidi Nepf, Prof. Herbert Einstein, and Prof. Louis Bucciarelli.","source_span":"Prof. Heidi Nepf\nProf. Herbert Einstein\nProf. Louis Bucciarelli"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-101-introduction-to-civil-and-environm_calendar","statement":"The course is offered by the Civil and Environmental Engineering department.","source_span":"Departments\nCivil and Environmental Engineering"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-101-introduction-to-civil-and-environm_calendar","statement":"Week 1 covers spreadsheets, units, significant figures, and uncertainty, taught by Bucciarelli and Einstein.","source_span":"Introduction to 1.101: Spreadsheets, units, significant figures, uncertainty, etc.\nPortfolio, lab book, report requirements.\nBucciarelli, Einstein"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-101-introduction-to-civil-and-environm_calendar","statement":"Week 2 introduces the Water Treatment Design Project and includes a lab measuring pH with a glass electrode sensor.","source_span":"Introduction to Water Treatment Design Project\nBucciarelli, Nepf\nMeasure pH with glass electrode sensor."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-101-introduction-to-civil-and-environm_calendar","statement":"Week 3 covers Porous Media: Packed Column Hydraulics, with labs measuring acid neutralization time and hydraulic conductivity.","source_span":"Porous Media: Packed Column Hydraulics\nNepf\nMeasure acid neutralization time. Measure hydraulic conductivity."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-101-introduction-to-civil-and-environm_calendar","statement":"Week 4's Acid/Base Chemistry lecture is given by Prof. Phil Gschwend.","source_span":"Acid/Base Chemistry\nProf. Phil Gschwend"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-101-introduction-to-civil-and-environm_calendar","statement":"Week 7 introduces the Structures Design Project and includes the Delta Design Exercise taught by Einstein.","source_span":"Introduction to Structures Design Project\nBucciarelli\nDelta Design Exercise\nEinstein"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-101-introduction-to-civil-and-environm_calendar","statement":"Week 8's lab includes a tension test, bending test, and strain gages.","source_span":"Tension test; bending test; strain gages."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-101-introduction-to-civil-and-environm_calendar","statement":"Week 12 introduces the Back Bay Design Project, taught by Einstein.","source_span":"Introduction to Back Bay Design Project\nEinstein"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-101-introduction-to-civil-and-environm_calendar","statement":"Week 14 includes oral presentations and reports due.","source_span":"Oral Presentations; Reports due"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-101-introduction-to-civil-and-environm_calendar","statement":"Week 15 includes a field trip and the portfolio is due.","source_span":"Field trip; Portfolio due"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_1-101-introduction-to-civil-and-environm_calendar","fact_refs":[1],"question":"Who are the three instructors for MIT course 1.101 in Fall 2006?","answer":"Prof. Heidi Nepf, Prof. Herbert Einstein, and Prof. Louis Bucciarelli."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_1-101-introduction-to-civil-and-environm_calendar","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What lab activity is done in Week 2 of 1.101?","answer":"Students measure pH using a glass electrode sensor."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_1-101-introduction-to-civil-and-environm_calendar","fact_refs":[6],"question":"Who gives the Week 4 lecture on Acid/Base Chemistry in 1.101?","answer":"Prof. Phil Gschwend."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_1-101-introduction-to-civil-and-environm_calendar","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What two measurements are made in the Week 3 lab on packed column hydraulics?","answer":"Acid neutralization time and hydraulic conductivity."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_1-101-introduction-to-civil-and-environm_calendar","fact_refs":[11],"question":"What happens during Week 15 of 1.101?","answer":"There is a field trip and the portfolio is due."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_1-101-introduction-to-civil-and-environm_calendar","fact_refs":[9],"question":"What design project is introduced in Week 12 of 1.101, and who teaches it?","answer":"The Back Bay Design Project, taught by Einstein."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-101-introduction-to-civil-and-environm_syllabus","statement":"Lectures meet 1 session per week for 1 hour, and labs meet 1 session per week for 3 hours.","source_span":"Lectures: 1 session / week, 1 hour / session\nLabs: 1 session / week, 3 hours / session"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-101-introduction-to-civil-and-environm_syllabus","statement":"The course involves three design tasks: water resources/treatment, structural design, and the conceptual redesign of Boston's Back Bay.","source_span":"a first concerning water resources/treatment, a second concerning structural design, and a third focusing on the conceptual (re)design of a large system, Boston's Back Bay"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-101-introduction-to-civil-and-environm_syllabus","statement":"Lab Section A meets Wednesday afternoon and Section B meets Thursday afternoon.","source_span":"Section A will meet on Wednesday afternoon, B on Thursday afternoon."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-101-introduction-to-civil-and-environm_syllabus","statement":"Students work in lab groups of 3 to 5.","source_span":"Students will work in groups of from 3 to 5 on all laboratory tasks."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-101-introduction-to-civil-and-environm_syllabus","statement":"Students must keep a lab notebook written in ink with no erasures.","source_span":"You are required to keep a lab notebook. You are to use ink, make no erasures."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-101-introduction-to-civil-and-environm_syllabus","statement":"Individual reports on each lab experiment are one to two pages long.","source_span":"You will prepare, as individuals, a one to two page report on each experiment done in the lab."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-101-introduction-to-civil-and-environm_syllabus","statement":"Grading is weighted: Design Task Reports and Portfolio 50%, Laboratory Experiments 30%, Active Participation 20%.","source_span":"Design Task Reports and Portfolio\n50%\nLaboratory Experiments\n30%\nActive Participation\n20%"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-101-introduction-to-civil-and-environm_syllabus","statement":"There is no final exam in the course.","source_span":"There is no final exam."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_1-101-introduction-to-civil-and-environm_syllabus","fact_refs":[0],"question":"How long are lecture and lab sessions in MIT's 1.101 course?","answer":"Lectures are 1 session per week lasting 1 hour, and labs are 1 session per week lasting 3 hours."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_1-101-introduction-to-civil-and-environm_syllabus","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What are the three design tasks students undertake in 1.101?","answer":"A water resources/treatment task, a structural design task, and a conceptual redesign of Boston's Back Bay."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_1-101-introduction-to-civil-and-environm_syllabus","fact_refs":[6,7],"question":"How is grading weighted in 1.101, and is there a final exam?","answer":"Design Task Reports and Portfolio count for 50%, Laboratory Experiments 30%, and Active Participation 20%; there is no final exam."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_1-101-introduction-to-civil-and-environm_syllabus","fact_refs":[3],"question":"How many students are in each lab group for 1.101?","answer":"Groups of 3 to 5 students."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_1-101-introduction-to-civil-and-environm_syllabus","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What are the rules for keeping the lab notebook in 1.101?","answer":"Students must use ink and make no erasures; faulty text is crossed out with a line rather than erased."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_1-101-introduction-to-civil-and-environm_syllabus","fact_refs":[2],"question":"On which days do Section A and Section B meet for labs in 1.101?","answer":"Section A meets Wednesday afternoon and Section B meets Thursday afternoon."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_10-01-ethics-for-engineers-artificial-in_assignments","statement":"Starting the second week of class, weekly writing assignments requiring paragraph answers to study questions are due at 11:59 pm the night before class.","source_span":"Each week starting the second week of class, students must answer each of the study questions in a paragraph or so... These assignments are due each week at 11:59 pm the night before class."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_10-01-ethics-for-engineers-artificial-in_assignments","statement":"Course version 6.9041 (12 units, Independent Inquiry) requires an additional 6 hours per week working on a project.","source_span":"For students in the 6.9041 version (12 units, Independent Inquiry), an additional 6 hours per week working on a project related to ethics and electical engineering / computer science is required."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_10-01-ethics-for-engineers-artificial-in_assignments","statement":"Version 6.9041 students must submit a 20-25 page paper by the last class.","source_span":"The student is expected to turn in a 20–25 page paper by the last class, in which the ethical and technical issues of a problem are analyzed."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_10-01-ethics-for-engineers-artificial-in_assignments","statement":"For version 6.9041, the final project counts for 50% of the final grade.","source_span":"Grading: the final project will count for 50% of the final grade."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_10-01-ethics-for-engineers-artificial-in_assignments","statement":"Course version 20.005 (9 units, BE restricted elective) requires an additional 3 hours per week working on a project.","source_span":"For students in the 20.005 version (9 units, BE restricted elective), an additional 3 hours per week working on a project related to ethics and BE is required."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_10-01-ethics-for-engineers-artificial-in_assignments","statement":"Version 20.005 students must submit a 10-15 page paper by the last class.","source_span":"The student is expected to turn in a 10–15 page paper by the last class, in which the ethical and technical issues of a problem are analyzed."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_10-01-ethics-for-engineers-artificial-in_assignments","statement":"For version 20.005, the final project counts for 33% of the final grade.","source_span":"Grading: the final project will count for 33% of the final grade."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_10-01-ethics-for-engineers-artificial-in_assignments","statement":"The 20.005 version paper should aim for roughly 50% technical and 50% ethical focus, with a 70/30 split in either direction acceptable.","source_span":"aiming roughly for 50% technical and 50% ethical focus, though we're flexible on exact proportions (70/30 in either direction is fine)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_10-01-ethics-for-engineers-artificial-in_assignments","statement":"The instructors for the course are Prof. Bernhardt Trout and Daniel Doneson.","source_span":"Prof. Bernhardt Trout\nDaniel Doneson"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_10-01-ethics-for-engineers-artificial-in_assignments","fact_refs":[0],"question":"When are weekly writing assignments due in the Ethics for Engineers: Artificial Intelligence course?","answer":"They are due at 11:59 pm the night before class, starting the second week of class."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_10-01-ethics-for-engineers-artificial-in_assignments","fact_refs":[1,2],"question":"How many extra hours per week must 6.9041 (12-unit) students spend on their project, and how long is the final paper?","answer":"6 extra hours per week, producing a 20-25 page final paper due by the last class."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_10-01-ethics-for-engineers-artificial-in_assignments","fact_refs":[3,6],"question":"What percentage of the final grade does the project count for in the 6.9041 version versus the 20.005 version?","answer":"The project counts for 50% of the final grade in the 6.9041 version and 33% in the 20.005 version."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_10-01-ethics-for-engineers-artificial-in_assignments","fact_refs":[5],"question":"How many pages should the 20.005 (9-unit) version project paper be?","answer":"10-15 pages."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_10-01-ethics-for-engineers-artificial-in_assignments","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What is the recommended technical/ethical balance for the 20.005 version project paper?","answer":"Roughly 50% technical and 50% ethical focus, though a 70/30 split in either direction is acceptable."}
